=== daylight_ending ===

= taking_the_truck

Gloria's truck. A Ford. Older model. Blue paint gone chalky from sun and dust. The cab smells like coffee grounds and vinyl.

You sit in the driver's seat. Adjust the mirror. Your face is in it. Yours. Close enough.

Through the windshield: the road. One road. It runs straight through Veinrock, past the motel, past the library, past the mine. The Hollows is at the far end, dark against the mountain, and even from here you can feel the hum in the steering wheel. In the dashboard. In the frame of the truck. The whole vehicle vibrates at a frequency too low to hear and too present to ignore.

The tank is full. Gloria filled it this morning. She knew you might need it. She knew before you did.

The truck is real. The road is real. The distance between here and the highway is real and measurable and you are going to drive every mile of it.

You pull out of the lot behind the diner. The gravel pops under the tires. The road opens in front of you.

You don't look in the rearview mirror.

-> the_drive


= the_drive

Desert. Road. Dark.

No other cars. The headlights cut two channels through the dark and the dark closes behind you. The road is straight for miles, then curves, then straight again. Joshua trees at the edge of the beams. Rock formations. Sand. Fifty miles from the mine and the landscape looks like nothing has ever happened to it.

The hum fades.

Not at a single moment. Not at a mile marker or a county line. You don't notice it going. At some point in the first hour you reach for the radio dial and your hand touches the steering wheel and the steering wheel is just a steering wheel. Plastic and metal. No vibration. No frequency. Just a thing your hands are holding because you're driving a truck through the desert in the dark.

You don't know when it stopped. You just know it's gone.

{has_mara_recording: Mara's recorder is in your jacket pocket. Day eight. Her voice on it, steady and precise, documenting what the mine was doing to her. Eight days. They thought she'd been on the ground for three.}

{has_mara_pages: Her research pages. Folded in your jacket. The carvings catalogued in her careful hand: containment, feeding, opening. The feeding cycles mapped across fifty years. The symbol taxonomy that took her ten days to build.}

{has_gloria_photograph: The photograph. The mine entrance, 1972. Martin Aldrich standing at the mouth with his hardhat and his lunch pail. The carvings visible in the rock face behind him. Before.}

{has_gloria_medallion: Martin Aldrich's disc. Heavy in your pocket. The containment symbol cast in metal by an engineer who understood the carvings. Gloria's last piece of her father that wasn't paper. She gave it to you. She gave it to a stranger because the stranger was leaving and she was not.}

The sun comes up around hour three. The sky goes from black to gray to a pale orange that fills the windshield and makes you squint. You haven't slept. Your eyes burn. The desert in daylight is flat and enormous and completely ordinary.

A gas station. Somewhere in Nevada. A different Nevada than the one you've been living in. This Nevada has a cashier who looks at you and sees you. Who says "morning" and means it. Who hands you change and the transaction is normal, is nothing, is the kind of interaction you'd forgotten was possible.

You fill the tank. You buy coffee. You get back in the truck.

You don't think about what you left behind. Not yet. The thinking will come later. Right now there is road and coffee and the sun coming up and the hum is gone and you are driving.

-> the_order


= the_order

The Order's Las Vegas field office is on the second floor of a medical plaza off Eastern Avenue. The directory in the lobby lists it as Pacific West Research Associates. The door looks like a dental practice. Frosted glass. Suite 204.

Inside: filing cabinets. Bookshelves. Stacked boxes. A desk with three monitors and a landline phone and a coffee mug holding pens. The overhead fluorescents are the same institutional frequency as every office everywhere. After six days of amber light and mine corridors, the normalcy is physical. A pressure change.

The receptionist looks up. Her eyes land on you and hold. She sees you.

"Can I help you?"

You give your name. Your agent designation. Active assignment, Veinrock, Nevada.

She picks up the phone.

Silas comes out of the back office. He sees you. His eyes find your face and there is a half-second pause. Recognition is there. Then it wavers. Not gone, but uncertain. He has your name but the face isn't quite matching.

"Val." He says it carefully. Testing the word. Then again, more certain: "Val."

He looks you over. Methodically. Eyes moving from your hands to your face to the way you're standing. Assessing the state of the source material.

Whatever he finds, it's better than he expected. You can see it in the way his shoulders settle. In the way the careful look eases.

"Sit down," he says. "Tell me everything."

-> the_debrief


= the_debrief

You sit across from Silas at a desk covered in files. The office smells like old paper and coffee. A ceiling fan turns slowly, clicking on every third rotation. Normal sounds. Normal light. Normal air.

You start from the beginning. Night zero. The motel. The hum. You talk and you lay the evidence out as you go, piece by piece, each one on the desk between you.

{has_mara_recording:
    Mara's recorder. You set it on the desk. Silas picks it up. Turns it over in his hands. Presses play.
    Her voice fills the office. Steady. Precise. The voice of someone documenting conditions in the field, the way she was trained to, the way all of you were trained to. Day eight.
    Silas listens to the whole thing. Doesn't interrupt. Doesn't take notes. Just listens, his eyes on the recorder, his hands still.
    When it finishes, he sets it down carefully.
    "Day eight." He looks at you. "We thought she'd been on the ground for three days."
}

{has_mara_pages:
    You unfold Mara's pages on the desk. The carvings catalogued in her hand. Containment, feeding, opening. The feeding cycles. The symbol taxonomy. The hand-drawn map of all three levels, the portal marked at the bottom.
    Silas reads slowly. His finger traces the symbol diagrams. He opens a drawer, pulls out a folder, matches Mara's taxonomy against the sketches the 1971 field agents filed. The same system. Recorded fifty years apart by two different investigators.
    "You found Mara's research." He says it without inflection. The implications are large enough that inflection would be inadequate.
}

{has_gloria_photograph:
    Silas holds the photograph up to the light. Martin Aldrich at the mine entrance. The rock face behind him. The carvings in the stone, intact, unbroken, framing the mouth of the mine.
    1972. One year before the sinkhole.
    "This is the before," Silas says. He sets the photograph next to Mara's pages. Before and after. The system working and the system broken.
}

{has_horrorco_filing: The HorrorCo incorporation document. Silas reads the date. June 22nd, 1973. One week after the sinkhole. One week after forty-eight men went into something that wasn't a collapse. "Someone expected the sinkhole." He puts the document down. "Or someone was waiting for it. The filing was prepared before the event. You don't incorporate a business in seven days."}

{has_sinkhole_history: Your notes on the 1973 Gazette. The county geological survey. The main shaft extending "beyond available sounding equipment." A shaft that went deeper than any instrument could measure, in a mine that had been operating for decades without going past eight hundred feet. Silas reads your notes. Reads them again. "That's consistent with an opening event. Not a collapse." He sets the notes down. "The shaft didn't extend. Something opened underneath it."}

{has_dale_file: You describe Dale's employee record. Twenty-seven identical performance reviews. Word for word. The same adjectives, the same numerical scores, repeated across twenty-seven years. Zero sick days. Zero vacation days. Zero absences of any kind. Silas is quiet for a moment. "That's not a person," he says. "That's a function."}

{has_census_data: Population data. You lay out the numbers. Veinrock, Nevada. Population zero in 1975, two years after the sinkhole. The town completely abandoned. Then: 47 in 1999. 54 in 2000. 54 in 2005. 54 in 2010. 54 in 2015. 54 in 2020. Nobody dies. Nobody leaves. Silas looks at the numbers. "A closed system," he says. "Nothing comes out. Nothing decays. The population only accumulates." He pauses. "That's not a town. That's a reservoir."}

{has_ritual_knowledge:
    You describe the containment pattern. The complete circuit. Not from notes. From memory. From the movement of your hands. You trace it on the desk with your finger: the arcs, the intersections, the sequence, the starting point, the direction each line runs. You describe the way it feels in your muscles. The way it lives below what the mine could reach.
    Silas records every detail. Audio. Sketches. Your finger's path on the desk, transcribed into a diagram.
    "If we get a team to the site," he says, "this is the protocol." He writes the word in the margin of his notes: CONTAINMENT. Underlines it.
}

{has_gloria_medallion:
    You take the disc out of your pocket and put it on the desk. Silas picks it up. Holds it next to the 1971 field agent sketches. The symbol on the disc. The sketches in the file. Same marks. Same system, cast in metal by an engineer who went into the mine every day for thirty years.
    "Where did you get this?"
    "A woman named Gloria. Her father wore it underground for thirty years. He was an engineer. He understood the carvings."
    Silas sets the disc down next to the sketches. Fifty years between the two objects. The symbol unchanged.
    "Her father."
    "Martin Aldrich."
}

You finish. The evidence is on the desk. Your voice is hoarse. The coffee in the mug Silas pushed toward you is cold.

Silas is quiet for a long time. He looks at the evidence. Looks at you. Back at the evidence.

"I believe you," he says. "Everything aligns. The 1971 report, Mara's research, the geological data, the population anomaly. Everything aligns."

He pauses. The pause is precise. Measured. Silas does not pause for effect. He pauses because the next sentence requires accuracy.

"But the Order needs corroboration. I need something I can point to that isn't testimony and isn't documents that could have been fabricated. I need a site."

You look at him.

"Look up Veinrock," you say.

-> the_blank_spot


= the_blank_spot

Silas turns to his monitor. Types.

The satellite image loads. You watch it render: the mountain range first, brown and gray ridgelines filling the screen from east to west. Then the resolution sharpens. The road appears. Highway 93, then the turnoff, then the county road that runs north through the desert toward the mountains.

The road runs straight through flat terrain. Then it reaches the base of the mountains.

And stops.

The town should be here. The intersection where the county road meets the mine road. The diner. The motel. The library. The parking lot. The mine entrance. Fifty-four people and a haunted attraction and a portal underneath it all.

Nothing. Rock and sand. Undifferentiated terrain. The road runs to the base of the mountains and the mountains are unbroken stone and there is nothing there. No buildings. No roads. No infrastructure. Nothing that has ever been built or inhabited or named.

Silas tries county GIS. No parcel data. No zoning records. No property boundaries. The county map shows unincorporated land, BLM designation, never surveyed.

He tries the post office. No delivery route. No zip code. No address on file for any location matching the coordinates.

He pulls up the census database. Types in Veinrock. No results. Searches by county, by coordinates, by any designation that might capture a community of fifty-four people living in the shadow of a mountain in the Nevada desert.

No results. No results. No results.

Silas sits back.

Veinrock is gone. The mine is gone. The attraction is gone. The town is gone. The diner where Gloria poured coffee and didn't ask questions. The motel where you slept and the mirror turned late. The library where the clerk watched you and then forgot you. All of it. Gone. Not destroyed. Not abandoned. Absent. The desert unbroken, as if nothing had ever been built there.

"The blank spot is the corroboration," Silas says. He says it quietly. He's not talking to you. He's talking to the evidence on his desk, to the recorder and the pages and the photographs of a place that no longer registers on any map or in any database. "You didn't have to prove the portal. The portal proved itself."

He looks at you.

"The mine reached capacity. Whatever the system was accumulating, it accumulated enough. The town was the container and the container collapsed inward." He's working through it. Analyst's reflex. "Everyone inside it went with it."

He pauses.

"Your partial erasure on arrival is its own evidence. The medical team has seen this before. Not at this scale. But the markers are the same." He looks at your hands. At the edges of you. "We can help. Some of the damage is reversible."

He doesn't say the rest. He doesn't have to. Some of it is not.

"The Order will investigate," he says. "I'll get a team to the coordinates. To where Veinrock used to be. The blank spot itself is measurable. The absence is data." He picks up his pen. Writes something on the folder. "This is enough, Val. This is enough."

The office is quiet. The ceiling fan clicks. Outside the frosted glass, the sounds of a medical plaza: footsteps, a distant phone, someone laughing in the hallway. The world continuing.

* "What about the people who were still inside?"

Silas sets the pen down.

"The woman who gave you the truck. Gloria."

"Yes."

"And the others. The guests. The staff. Fifty-four residents."

"Yes."

Silas looks at the satellite image. The blank spot. The unbroken terrain where a town used to be.

"The blank spot on the map is probably the answer," he says. He says it carefully, precisely, without softening. "If the town collapsed inward, the people inside it collapsed with it. That's consistent with every model we have for this type of event."

He's quiet.

"I'm sorry, Val."

-> daylight_end


= daylight_end

You walk out of the Order's field office. Down the stairs. Through the lobby. Through the glass door and into the parking lot.

Las Vegas afternoon. Exhaust and heat and traffic. A plane descending toward McCarran. A billboard for a casino buffet. The sound of air conditioning units and car horns and a radio playing from an open window across the street. The world continuing without knowledge of what opened under a mine in the Nevada desert. Without knowledge that a town was there and then was not.

You survived. You brought evidence. You gave the Order what they needed. Silas believed you. The blank spot on the map is the corroboration. The investigation will continue. A team will go to the coordinates. The protocol is in Silas's notes. The evidence is in Silas's files.

{has_mara_recording: Mara's recorder is with Silas now. Day eight. Her voice in a filing cabinet in Suite 204 of a medical plaza off Eastern Avenue.}

{has_gloria_photograph: The photograph is with Silas now. Martin Aldrich at the mine entrance. 1972. The carvings intact. The before. Filed as evidence in a case that will be assigned a number and a classification and a folder.}

{has_gloria_medallion: The medallion is with Silas now. Gloria's last piece of her father. The containment symbol he wore underground for thirty years, cast in metal by hands that understood the carvings. Given to a stranger, filed as evidence, catalogued and stored. Gloria's inheritance, reduced to a data point in an Order report.}

Gloria stayed. She knew what the town was. She knew what the mine was doing. She'd known for decades. Her father knew. The fifty-four people who lived in Veinrock's permanent population knew or didn't know or chose not to know. She filled the tank. She left the keys. She gave you the truck and she stayed.

The blank spot on the map is the corroboration.

The blank spot on the map is also where everyone was.

You find Gloria's truck in the parking lot. The blue Ford with the chalky paint and the vinyl seats and the floor mat where the keys were. You unlock it. You sit in the driver's seat. You don't start the engine.

The steering wheel under your hands. No hum. No vibration. Just a truck in a parking lot in Las Vegas in the afternoon heat. Just a vehicle that belongs to a woman who no longer exists on any map, in any database, in any record the world keeps of the people who live in it.

Gloria's truck. She kept it maintained.

You sit for a while.

Then you start the engine.

-> DONE
